WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.600
A passing check can still be wrong.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.520
Here is a change to a program, and here are its tests.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.440
Every test passes.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.520
The change is still wrong.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.120
None of the tests ever runs the line that broke.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.960
A pass tells you the check found nothing.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.880
It does not tell you the check could have found it.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.720
A common stand-in for a good test suite is coverage: how much of the code the tests
run.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:34.220
Laura Inozemtseva and Reid Holmes built thirty-one thousand test suites for five
large Java programs and measured how many planted faults each one caught.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:40.020
Once they allowed for the size of a suite, coverage was only weakly to moderately
related to catching faults.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.400
In one program the link was essentially zero.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.020
Their advice: coverage should not be used as a quality target.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.860
One way to test a test is to break the code on purpose and see whether the tests
notice.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.180
The deliberate breaks are called mutants.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:02.400
Rene Just and colleagues took three hundred and fifty-seven real bugs that
developers had fixed, and asked whether mutants behave like them.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.200
Seventy-three percent of the real bugs were coupled to mutants.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.640
Seventeen percent were coupled to none, so a check on the check has blind spots of
its own.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.420
Coding benchmarks for AI count a task as solved when the patch passes the task's
tests.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.580
A 2025 study re-examined patches from three tools on five hundred tasks.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:31.700
Seven point eight percent of the patches counted as solved failed the developers'
own tests, and twenty-nine point six percent behaved differently from the real fix.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.540
An earlier study hand-checked two hundred and fifty-one passing patches from one
agent.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:42.780
In about a third, the fix was already written in the task description, and about
another third passed weak tests.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.840
Both studies are preprints.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.680
They show how far a pass can sit from a fix, on these benchmarks.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.780
So before trusting a check, feed it something you know is wrong.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.700
If a broken input passes, the check is broken.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:01.660
That planted failure is a false-success control, and it costs one extra run.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.440
A pass with a control that failed as it should is evidence.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.380
A pass on its own is only the absence of a complaint.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.160
A check earns trust by failing on something it should fail on, and doing it on the
record.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.700
The voice you heard is a synthesized version of mine.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.200
Every number on screen links to its source under the video.
